From 5d6cc0ce34a91aa3c4a12afe37cdac4faf812442 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mohammed <91819504+mohammed7s@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 00:11:04 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] computercontrol: consult a user allowlist next to
 DeviceConfig

Computer Control decides which apps may own a session and which apps may
be automated from the DeviceConfig keys allowed_session_owner_apps and
allowed_automatable_apps (namespace computer_control). Devices that do not
receive DeviceConfig have no way to use the feature, and on devices that
do, the vendor rather than the user decides.

Add a per-user allowlist for both roles and consult it alongside the
DeviceConfig lists, in the same two places:

 - isPackageApprovedToRunAutomation(): the session owner check, reached
   from session creation after the ACCESS_COMPUTER_CONTROL, notification
   and assistant role checks;
 - isPackageInAutomatableAppList(): the target check, reached from
   session creation and from every launchApplication(). An app the user
   approved as an agent can never be a target, and the DeviceConfig
   denylist still wins.

The lists are empty by default and nothing in this change adds to them.
Entries are bound to the signing certificates the package had when the
user allowed it, so a differently signed package with the same name does
not inherit them; a rotated key with the old certificate in its lineage
does. They are kept in /data/system/computercontrol/user_allowlist.xml,
read once at boot (a corrupt file reads as empty), dropped when the
package is uninstalled and shown in dumpsys virtualdevice.

The assistant role requirement, per-app consent, the consent dialog, test
agents and the DeviceConfig path behave as before.

Test: atest ComputerControlUserAllowlistTest ComputerControlAllowlistControllerTest
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
